/* std/frame: a physical frame as a capability (kernel stage 18, MK10-S1's
   second half, `kelp wiki page stdlib/irq.md`).

   A physical frame is a 4 KiB-aligned page a driver may own. Like a `Line`,
   the frame is a *value* and `claim` is the only constructor: the compiler
   checks the address against the calling module's `frames` grant at the call,
   refuses a constant that is not `4096`-aligned, and refuses a misaligned
   address at run time with a message. A constant address has to fall inside a
   granted range, and a computed one is refused unless the grant is `["*"]`.

       datac Frame(val addr: usize)

       fun claim(addr: usize) => Frame
       fun Frame.addr() => usize
       fun Frame.offset(byte: usize) => usize

   `offset` is the only way this module hands an address back out, and it is
   bounds-checked against the frame's own size, so a caller cannot walk out of
   the frame by asking for byte 4096. What it cannot do is stop the caller from
   walking on from the returned *number*: an offset applied later is the same
   arithmetic the `mmio` check already declines to revisit
   (`kelp wiki page language/capabilities.md`), and DMA is not tracked at all.

   A hosted program may import this module and claim a frame as well: the check
   is a compile-time one, but a hosted program has no physical frames of its
   own, so a frame it "claims" is only the address the compiler admitted.
   Freestanding is where the address names something. */

datac Frame(val addr: usize)

// The receipt for a `frames` grant: a frame at a 4 KiB-aligned address the
// calling module was granted. The alignment is checked again here, because a
// computed address is admitted under the `["*"]` grant and the compiler cannot
// fold it.
fun claim(addr: usize) => Frame {
    if addr % 4096 != 0 {
        panic("Frame.claim: address is not 4096-aligned")
    }
    return Frame(addr = addr)
}

// The frame's address.
fun Frame.addr() => usize = addr

// The address `byte` bytes into the frame. The byte is bounds-checked against
// the frame's own 4096 bytes, so this cannot leave the frame; the returned
// address is a plain `usize`, and nothing here says what is mapped there.
fun Frame.offset(byte: usize) => usize {
    if byte >= 4096 {
        panic("Frame.offset: byte is outside the frame")
    }
    return addr + byte
}
